data.content:
Your code **doesn't use `Thread.Abort`**, so there's no migration issue here. However, the pattern itself (manual `Thread` creation + `Join`) is outdated. The modern .NET 8 equivalent:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        await Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, async (item, ct) =>
        {
            await Task.Run(() => Process(item), ct);
        });
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item)
    {
        Thread.Sleep(100); // consider await Task.Delay(100) if async
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

**Key `Thread.Abort` migration guidance** (for when you do encounter it):

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` + cooperative cancellation |
| `ThreadAbortException` catch | No equivalent — remove these catches |
| Timeout-based abort | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter()` |
| Hard kill of runaway work | `Process`-level isolation (out-of-proc) |

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The fix is always **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.
